What I actually checked:

Ran the feature both ways. Under a pty, yoyo --restricted printed all three note lines — safe-mode reuse, file tools fenced to the working directory: /tmp/restr_probe, and the anti-false-confinement command execution is NOT disabled — use --read or --no-tools for that. --help shows the entry with no value placeholder (so the #862 gate is satisfied). Without the flag, nothing new prints. The earlier piped run showed no note, which is correct rather than a bug: piped stdin+stdout auto-enables quiet, and the note is deliberately silent there.

**The touchpoint that could have silently broken it.** `main.rs` dropped `dir_restrictions` whenever safe mode was on — and `--restricted` turns safe mode on, so without the `&& !cli::is_restricted() guard the flag would have fenced and then immediately un-fenced itself. That guard, plus set_restricted being added to GLOBAL_SETTERS, are both in this diff. The three-arm match on RestrictedDirOutcome has no _` catch-all, so a future variant is a compile error rather than a silent fall-through.

**The safety property holds.** The near-miss guard asserts a user-supplied allow list survives byte-identically with a full `assert_eq!`, not a `contains` — so no one who already passed --allow-dir gets widened. CwdUnresolved is kept distinct from Fenced and its note says NOT fenced outright.

Call-site placement deviates from the task text and is better for it. The task said "before parse_permission_and_dir_config"; the code puts clause B after it, with a comment explaining that the monotonicity rule must see the fully-resolved CLI+config allow list or it could append cwd on top of a non-empty config list and widen the user. Clause A is still read at the top, and the live "Safe mode" banner confirms it reaches the gates.

